{
  "id": "proj-bat-house",
  "slug": "bat-house",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Bat House",
  "summary": "A single-chamber bat box that eats your mosquito problem. Rough-sawn interior for grip.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 2,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Cut three 24 in wide pieces from the plywood with the good face out: the back at 26 1/2 in, the upper front at 16 1/2 in and the lower front at 5 in. Cut the furring strip into one 24 in top spacer and two 20 1/2 in side spacers, and cut the roof from the 1x4 at 28 in. These are Bat Conservation International's single-chamber dimensions.",
    "Saw shallow grooves across the inside of the back from top to bottom, 1/4 in to 1/2 in apart and 1/32 in to 1/16 in deep, so the bats can grip. Do not cut deeper or the plywood weakens.",
    "Brush two coats of dark water-based stain on the inside faces of the back and both front pieces. Do not paint the inside, because paint fills the grooves.",
    "Run a bead of caulk under each spacer and screw the top spacer along the top of the back and the side spacers down each edge. The bottom stays open as the entrance, which makes the chamber 3/4 in deep.",
    "Caulk the spacers. Screw on the upper front flush with the top, leave a 1/2 in vent gap, then screw on the lower front. The back extends 4 1/2 in below the front as the landing area.",
    "Screw the roof on flush with the back so it overhangs the front and the sides. Caulk every outside joint.",
    "Prime the outside and give it two coats of exterior paint. Use a darker colour in cooler climates and a lighter one where summers are hot.",
    "Mount it 12 ft or higher, ideally 15 to 20 ft, on a building wall that gets morning or all-day sun, clear of branches and lights. Drive two 3 in screws near the top and two through the landing area into the wall framing. Put it up before spring."
  ],
